speechtext:
And, Sir Sanford Fleming also reported ;
The settlement of the province has naturally enough followed its navigable waters ; on the south by the Bay of Fundy and its inlets ; on the east by the coast and bays of the Gulf of St. Lawrence ; on the west by the river St. John which extends and to some extent is navigable almost to the extreme north-westerly angle of the province. In consequence, New Brunswick may be said to be populated as yet only along its outskirts. There is a vast area in the interior unoccupied, not because the soil is so much more uncultivable than elsewhere, but because it has hitherto been, and is still, inaccessible.
That condition of affairs to a very large extent prevails to-day, although a great many years have passed since that report was prepared. In the lower part of the province the Canada Eastern line stretches from the River St. John across to the gulf, and there are here and there one or two small branch lines; but practically the great part of the Interior of the province of New Brunswick described in Sir Sanford Fleming's report of that day is still inaccessible and not open to settlement; and the reason why it lias not been settled has been, to use Sir Sanford Fleming's own words, ' not because the soil is so much uncultivable than elsewhere, but because it has hitherto been and still is inaccessible.'
